  "image_name": "8dcbf21e5794119691fad548524fa12d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a coupled iron redox cycle in a microbial environment and shows how several metabolisms feed into it. A large central loop links Fe(II) and Fe(III): Fe(II) is oxidized to Fe(III) near the top of the cycle, while Fe(III) is reduced back to Fe(II) along the bottom.\n\nSeveral visible pathways drive iron oxidation. Oxygenic photosynthesis produces O2 and reactive oxygen species, which appear to promote Fe(II) oxidation, and microaerophiles may do the same under low oxygen. Photoferrotrophy is shown as a direct biological route oxidizing Fe(II). Nitrate-dependent Fe oxidation (NRFeOx) is another major branch, tied to nitrate and nitrite and producing various nitrogen products such as NO, N2O, N2, DON, and NH4.\n\nIron reduction is linked to heterotrophic or abiotic processes. Fe(III) reduction uses H2 or organic carbon, and superoxide also appears to contribute to reduction. Fe(III) photoreduction and reactions involving organic matter return Fe(III) to Fe(II) as well.\n\nThe diagram also connects iron cycling to manganese and sulfur chemistry. A manganese cycle involving MnOx and Mn2+ interacts with iron oxidation, while a “cryptic” sulfur cycle links Fe(III) to H2S, elemental and intermediate sulfur species, sulfate, and FeS formation. Overall, the image emphasizes a tightly interconnected Fe cycle coupled to O, N, Mn, and S transformations.",
